/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Water circulates.
   - For each device the steady-flow energy balance, with kinetic and potential energy neglected, gives q − w = Δh.
   - A Carnot cycle between the same temperatures would be more efficient, but it cannot be built for steam: it would have to compress a wet mixture.
   - On the T–s diagram of the figure the saturated liquid and vapour lines form the dome.
   - The thermal efficiency compares net work out with heat paid for in the boiler.
   - η is the cycle thermal efficiency (dimensionless); h₃ is turbine-inlet enthalpy, h₄ turbine-exit enthalpy, h₁ condenser-exit (pump-inlet) enthalpy, h₂ pump-exit (boiler-inlet) enthalpy, all in kJ/kg.
   - Real machines fall short.
   - Typical large utility values: boiler at 100 bar and 540 °C, condenser at 0.1 bar, where the saturation temperature is 45.8 °C.
   - Raising boiler pressure also raises efficiency, but it pushes the turbine exhaust deeper into the wet region, so higher pressure goes together with superheat and reheat.
*/
